.TH CCOL_FIXED_WIDTH_DATA_TYPE_SIZE 3 "2026-09-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size \- byte size of the C type that a ccol_data_type names
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/common.h>

size_t ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(ccol_data_type type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
For every ccol_data_type that names one specific C type, this returns
sizeof() of that type. ccol_string, ccol_other_types and any value the
function doesn't recognize return 0, since a key or a value of those
types can legitimately have any size and there is no single width to
report.

For a fixed-width key type, a key_pair must carry exactly this width,
because every chmap and cbmap entry point that takes such a pair
requires it. Code that builds a cmap_pair by hand can call this function
to give the pair the right size.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
type   The data type enumerator whose size you want
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
sizeof() of the C type that matches the enumerator, or 0 when the type
has no fixed width, which is the case for ccol_string, ccol_other_types
and any value that the function does not recognize.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
common.h defines this as a static inline function, so the library
exports no symbol for it.
The width is the sizeof() of the platform you build for, so for long, a
pointer and long double it changes with the ABI.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
size_t width = ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(ccol_int);
int key = 42;
cmap_pair kp = { .ptr = &key, .size = width };
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_insert_elem (3)
